<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edo (Tokyo)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edo, renamed Tokyo in 1868, was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> made it his base, and it grew into one of the largest cities in the world, partly because the alternate attendance system required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> to spend time there. It remained the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s government until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo.jpg" alt="Edo (Tokyo)" width="320" height="145" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; stitched by Marku1988 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edo_P.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan imported roughly 80 per cent of its oil from the United States, so the American oil embargo of August 1941 pushed <b>Tokyo</b> towards attacking Pearl Harbor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of China grew out of the revolutionary movement against the Qing Empire led by Sun Yat-sen, whose Tongmenghui, or Revolutionary Alliance, was founded in <b>Tokyo</b> in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1879 Matsuda Michiyuki arrived at Shuri with police and troops, the domain was abolished and replaced by Okinawa Prefecture, and Sho Tai was removed to <b>Tokyo</b>, where he was later made a marquis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kokura lay at the end of the Nagasaki road, where Dutch missions travelling to <b>Edo</b> crossed the straits to Honshu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kokura-domain">Kokura Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After its surrender Ujimasa was ordered to kill himself and Ujinao was exiled; their lands went to Tokugawa Ieyasu, who made <b>Edo</b> his base.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>It disbanded the armed forces, tried war leaders at the <b>Tokyo</b> tribunal, purged officials, broke up the zaibatsu, carried out a sweeping land reform, gave women the vote and in 1947 introduced a new constitution renouncing war.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-japan">Occupied Japan</a></li></ul></section>
